'''anta'''<sup>1</sup> (an′tä̤), ''n.''; pl. ''antæ'' (-tē). [L., a terminal pilaster, < ''ante'', before: see ''ante-''.] In ''arch.'',
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Anta.}}}}
{{c|{{smaller|Elevation and Plan of Portico in Antis, Temple of Themis, Rhamnus. A, A, antæ; ''a'', ''a'', antæ opposite pillars; B, pronaos; C, crepidoma.}}}}
a pilaster, especially a pilaster in certain positions, as one of a pair on either side of a doorway, or one standing opposite a pillar; specifically, the pilaster used in Greek and Roman architecture to terminate one of the side walls of a building when these are prolonged beyond the face of the end wall. {{smaller|A portico ''in antis'' (that is, between antæ) is formed when the side walls are thus prolonged and columns stand between the antæ.}}

'''anta'''<sup>2</sup> (an′tä̤), ''n.'' [Sp. Pg. ''anta'', < Braz. ''anta''.] The native Brazilian name of the common or American tapir, ''Tapirus americanus''.

